        "title": "Historical Geography and the Feudal World - Overview",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "### Problem 1: Personal Union\nA single person holds multiple titles inherited or won separately.\n```\nEdward III of England (r. 1327–1377):\n  King of England\n  Duke of Aquitaine (as vassal of France)\n  Claimant to the throne of France (through his mother Isabella)\n  → This contradiction triggered the Hundred Years War\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#030",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:historical-geography:00-overview, git-history:historical-geography:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "historical-geography/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "historical-geography/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:historical-geography:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "historical-geography",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "historical-geography",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Historical Geography and the Feudal World - Overview",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "### Problem 2: Sub-infeudation\nA lord grants a fief to a vassal who grants sub-fiefs to sub-vassals.\nThe grantor of the sub-fief now stands between the king and the sub-vassal.\nQuia Emptores (1290, England) stopped this — but only in England."
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#031",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
